<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Intentionally EMPTY (#211).

    Unlike che-ical-mcp (which needs com.apple.security.personal-information.
    calendars/reminders so macOS 26.5 will show the Calendar/Reminders TCC
    prompt under hardened runtime), this server's only TCC dependency is
    **Full Disk Access** (kTCCServiceSystemPolicyAllFiles).

    FDA has NO corresponding entitlement key — there is no
    "personal-information.full-disk-access". FDA is granted purely by the user
    in System Settings; the binary needs no entitlement to benefit from it.
    A hardened-runtime signature with no extra entitlements is sufficient: once
    the user grants FDA, the process reads ~/Library/Mail with no sandbox in
    the way.

    Why ship an empty file at all: it makes the "no special entitlements"
    decision explicit and gives scripts/sign-and-notarize.sh a stable, optional
    --entitlements target. The point of signing here is the **stable Developer
    ID designated requirement**, which makes TCC bind the FDA grant to the
    signing identity (not the binary's cdhash) so it survives version bumps.

    If a future feature ever needs a real entitlement, add it here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
